Input: A graph \( G \) with \( sc(u, v) \) for each edge \( (u, v) \)
Output: The hierarchy tree of ECo-decomposition of \( G \)

Initialize an empty hierarchy tree \( \mathcal{T} \);
for each vertex \( u \in V(G) \) do Insert a singleton node \( u \) into \( \mathcal{T} \);
for each edge \( (u, v) \in E(G) \) in non-increasing \( sc(u, v) \) order do
    Let \( r_u \) (resp \( r_v \)) be the root of the tree in \( \mathcal{T} \) containing \( u \) (resp \( v \));
    if \( r_u = r_v \) then continue;
    else if both \( r_u \) and \( r_v \) are ECC nodes with weight \( sc(u, v) \) then
        Merge \( r_u \) and \( r_v \) into a single ECC node;
    else if none of \( r_u \) or \( r_v \) is an ECC node with weight \( sc(u, v) \) then
        Create a new ECC node in \( \mathcal{T} \) with weight \( sc(u, v) \), and add \( r_u \) and \( r_v \) as its children;
    else
        Without loss of generality, assume \( r_u \) is an ECC node with weight \( sc(u, v) \), and add \( r_v \) as a child of \( r_u \) in \( \mathcal{T} \);
